Over the years, people suggested almost everything. Hurricane-force winds. Slippery slime made by tiny [[algae|algae]]. Magnetism. Pranksters dragging rocks at night. A few even blamed aliens.

In the 1970s, two [[geologist|geologists]] named dozens of rocks, gave them nicknames such as Karen, and mapped where they moved over seven years. Karen, a block weighing about 300 kg, did not budge during their study, while much smaller rocks wandered. The trouble was that nobody ever saw a rock in motion. The moves happened rarely, perhaps once every few years, and the playa is remote and empty.

{{story:gps-how}}

ज़्यादातर लोग थार को सुनहरे [[@deserts-sand|रेत के टीलों]] के रूप में सोचते हैं, और सच में बड़े हिस्से पर [[@earth-weird-moving-dunes|टीले]] छाए हैं। कुछ लंबी मेड़ों जैसे हैं, तो कुछ अर्धचंद्र जैसे, जो हवा के साथ खिसकते रहते हैं। जैसलमेर के पास सम के टीलों पर लोग शाम ढले ऊँट की सवारी करने आते हैं।

पर थार में पथरीली पहाड़ियाँ, खारी झीलें और कंकड़ से भरे चौड़े मैदान भी हैं। मानसून से पहले तेज़ हवाएँ ढेर सारी धूल उड़ाती हैं। इसमें से कुछ दिल्ली तक पहुँच जाती है और आसमान धुँधला नारंगी हो जाता है। गर्मियों में फलोदी और जैसलमेर जैसी जगहों पर ज़मीन 45 °C से भी कहीं ज़्यादा तपती है।

{{story:camels}}

The mushroom you see is a bit like an apple on a tree. It is the part that makes and releases spores, the fungus version of seeds. The real body is hidden: a mass of fine white threads called [[mycelium|mycelium]] spreading through soil or wood.

Those threads can grow enormous. In the Malheur National Forest in Oregon, USA, one honey fungus covers about 9 square kilometres, an area bigger than 1,200 football fields. Scientists think it could be thousands of years old. From above, all you would notice is a scattering of mushrooms and some dying trees.

{{story:antibiotics}}

Ice is always melting and freezing a little at its surface. At 0 °C the two happen at the same rate, so nothing seems to change. Salt dissolves in the thin layer of water on the ice. Now that water can't refreeze easily, but the ice keeps melting.

Melting needs energy. Ice takes it as heat from whatever it touches: the air, the bucket and the tub of cream. So the temperature keeps falling until the salty water reaches its own, much lower, freezing point. It's the same science that helps [[@salt-ice-roads|salt melt ice on roads]] in snowy places like Chicago and Shimla.

{{story:mughals}}

लगभग 1910 में कुछ चित्रकारों ने ऐसी तस्वीरें बनाईं जिनमें कोई चीज़ थी ही नहीं। इसे [[abstract|अमूर्त]] कला कहते हैं। रूस के चित्रकार वासिली कैंडिंस्की मानते थे कि रंग और आकार संगीत की तरह काम कर सकते हैं, यानी बिना कोई कहानी सुनाए [[@emotions|भावनाएँ]] जगा सकते हैं।

हॉलैंड के चित्रकार पीट मॉन्ड्रियन ने अपनी तस्वीरों को बस काली रेखाओं और लाल, पीले और नीले खानों तक सीमित कर दिया। 1940 के दशक में अमेरिका में जैक्सन पोलक विशाल कैनवस फ़र्श पर बिछा देते थे और उन पर रंग टपकाते और छिड़कते हुए किसी नर्तक की तरह इधर-उधर घूमते थे। नतीजा जितना एक तस्वीर था, उतना ही हरकत का एक रिकॉर्ड भी।

{{story:emotions}}

कर्नाटक संगीत सुरों के बीच फिसलन, लहर और कंपकंपी से भरा है, जिन्हें *गमक* कहते हैं। गायक इन्हें आवाज़ से करता है। वीणा बजाने वाली इन्हें तार को पर्दे पर बगल की ओर खींचकर करती है, जिससे तार कसता है और सुर ऊपर जाता है।

पर्दे चिकने हैं और काफ़ी ऊँचे जमे होते हैं, इसलिए बजाने वाली एक ही सुर को कई सीढ़ी ऊपर तक मोड़ सकती है। यही एक वजह है कि वीणा इंसानी आवाज़ के इतने क़रीब है, और कर्नाटक संगीत के बहुत से महान गायकों ने वीणा भी सीखी। यह [[@carnatic-hindustani|कर्नाटक संगीत]] की शैली पर बिल्कुल सटीक बैठती है।

{{story:gupta-golden-age}}

In 1883, Germany's chancellor Otto von Bismarck brought in compulsory health insurance for many workers, paid for by both workers and employers. It was among the first national schemes of its kind, and he was partly trying to win support away from socialist rivals. Many European countries and Japan now run systems based on this idea.

In 1948, after the Second World War, Britain's health minister Aneurin Bevan launched the National Health Service. It was paid for out of taxes, and anyone could [[@jobs-doctor|see a doctor]] or go to [[@hospitals|hospital]] without paying at the door. Some doctors opposed it at first. It is now one of the largest employers in the world.

{{story:doctors-history}}

After [[@partition|Partition]] in 1947, Punjab lost its old capital, Lahore, to Pakistan. India decided to build a new one. The Swiss-French [[@jobs-architect|architect]] Le Corbusier took charge in the early 1950s, and [[@chandigarh|Chandigarh]] was the result.

The city is divided into sectors, each about 800 m by 1,200 m. Every sector was meant to be a small neighbourhood with its own shops, school and green spaces, so daily needs were within walking distance. Fast roads run around the sectors, not through them. Chandigarh was also planned with large green belts, a long park, Leisure Valley, and a man-made lake, Sukhna, at its edge. Today it is often ranked among the cleanest and greenest cities in India, though it has grown far beyond the number of people it was first planned for.

{{story:chandigarh}}

Over in Manipur, the hero was Ngangom Dingko Singh, who won gold at the 1998 Asian Games in Bangkok. A teenage girl from a farming family, Mangte Chungneijang Mary Kom, was inspired by him and started training soon after.

Mary Kom became one of the most successful amateur boxers ever. She won six world titles between 2002 and 2018, an Olympic bronze, and a [[@commonwealth-games|Commonwealth Games]] gold in 2018. She fought in lighter weight classes, often against taller opponents, and won with speed and clever footwork. A Hindi film about her life came out in 2014.

{{story:reflexes}}

Historians believe cards reached Europe from the Mamluk Sultanate, which ruled Egypt and Syria. A beautiful Mamluk pack from about the 15th century survives in a museum in Istanbul.

European records suddenly mention cards in the late 1370s, often because cities were banning them as a form of gambling. Italian and Spanish packs kept suits close to the Mamluk ones: cups, coins, swords and batons. German makers chose hearts, bells, acorns and leaves. In France, around 1480, card makers settled on the simple symbols most of the world now uses.

{{story:printing-press}}

Every January, musicians gather at Thiruvaiyaru in Tamil Nadu, on the banks of the Kaveri, for the Thyagaraja Aradhana. It honours Thyagaraja, one of the great composers of [[@carnatic-hindustani|Carnatic music]], who died there in 1847.

The highlight comes when hundreds of singers and instrumentalists sit together and perform his five Pancharatna kritis, or "five gems", in one huge group. Famous performers sit beside students. Thousands of listeners come, many sitting on the sand.

{{story:sound-travel}}

After many attempts, Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa climber living in Darjeeling, and Edmund Hillary from New Zealand reached the summit of [[@everest-first|Everest]] on 29 May 1953. They stayed about 15 minutes. Tenzing left small offerings of sweets in the snow, a gift to the mountain.

In 1984 Bachendri Pal became the first Indian woman to climb Everest. In 2013 Arunima Sinha, a former national volleyball player who had lost a leg in a train attack, became the first woman amputee to stand on top.

{{story:lungs-breathe}}

For years women's cricket in India got little attention. [[@mithali-raj|Mithali Raj]] helped change that. She scored more runs in women's one-day internationals than any other player and led India to two World Cup finals, in 2005 and 2017, losing both.

On 2 November 2025, at the DY Patil Stadium in Navi Mumbai, India finally won. Captained by Harmanpreet Kaur, the team beat South Africa by 52 runs in the final. Fans celebrated late into the night across the country, and the players became household names.

{{story:sports-science}}

जब सूखी ज़मीन पर बारिश गिरती है, तो उठने वाली सोंधी गंध का एक नाम है: *पेट्रिकोर*। दो ऑस्ट्रेलियाई वैज्ञानिकों, इसाबेल बेयर और रिचर्ड थॉमस ने 1964 में यह नाम गढ़ा। इस गंध का कुछ हिस्सा जियोस्मिन नाम के एक रसायन से आता है, जिसे [[@soil-made|मिट्टी]] में रहने वाले [[bacteria|बैक्टीरिया]] बनाते हैं। हवा में जियोस्मिन का ज़रा-सा अंश भी हो, तब भी इंसानी नाक उसे पकड़ लेती है।

उत्तर प्रदेश के कन्नौज में इत्र बनाने वाले लंबे समय से *मिट्टी अत्तर* बनाते आए हैं, यानी "मिट्टी का इत्र"। वे सूखे तालाबों की मिट्टी की टिकियाँ पकाते हैं और [[distillation|आसवन]] से उसकी महक निकालते हैं, यानी [[@monsoon|मानसून]] की पहली बौछार की गंध शीशी में बंद।

{{story:monsoon}}

Online, anyone can pick a name and a photo. Someone who claims to be 12 might be much older. Most people are friendly, but a few try to win a child's trust, then ask for photos, secrets or a meeting.

Clear rules help. Don't meet anyone from online in person without a parent's permission and company. If someone asks you to keep your chats secret, sends things that upset you, or pressures you to share pictures, stop replying and tell a trusted adult. You won't be in trouble for telling. Our article on [[@friendship|friendship]] looks at what real friends do.

{{story:internet-citizens}}

In 1855, Naoroji moved to England to work for an Indian trading firm. Living there, he started asking a hard question. Why was India so poor when it produced so much?

His answer became known as the drain theory. India paid for British officials' salaries and pensions, for an army it did not control and for goods it was forced to buy. Much of that money left the country for good. He calculated India's income per person and showed it was tiny. These ideas filled his 1901 book, *Poverty and Un-British Rule in India*. The [[@east-india-company|East India Company]] had once run India for profit. Naoroji argued that the British government still did.

{{story:london}}

For 90 years, the world record was 58 °C, measured at El Azizia in Libya in 1922. In 2012, a panel of experts from the World Meteorological Organization re-examined it.

They found several problems. The observer was new to the job, the instrument was a type that is easy to misread, and the reading did not match nearby places. The panel threw the record out, and Death Valley took over. A similar thing happened in India in 2024, when a weather station in Delhi reported 52.9 °C. The India Meteorological Department checked it and found the [[sensor|sensor]] was faulty.

{{story:sweat}}

Around the year 1010, the [[@cholas|Chola]] king Rajaraja I completed the [[@india-wonder-brihadeeswara|Brihadeeswarar Temple]] at Thanjavur. Its main tower, called a vimana, rises about 66 m over the shrine of Shiva. For its time, it was one of the tallest buildings in India, made mostly of [[granite|granite]], a very hard stone.

At the top sits a massive capstone. A popular story says it was hauled up a long earth ramp, but no clear evidence for the ramp has been found. Some experts think scaffolding and earth slopes were used together, while others suggest ramps built around the tower itself. The temple is still in use, a thousand years later. With two other Chola temples, it forms a UNESCO World Heritage Site called the Great Living Chola Temples.

{{story:cholas}}

No one adds [[yeast|yeast]] to idli batter. The microbes are already there, on the rice and dal, on the [[@how-mixer-grinder|grinder]] and on the cook's hands. Scientists who studied idli batter found that one group of lactic acid bacteria grows fastest and makes most of the gas.

As the bacteria multiply, the batter becomes more acidic. Many harmful germs cannot live in acid, so the [[@fermentation|fermentation]] also helps keep the batter safe. It works best between about 25 and 35°C, which is why idli comes so easily in a hot South Indian kitchen.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}

The most famous laddoo in India is made at the Tirumala temple near Tirupati in Andhra Pradesh. Records say the temple started offering laddoos to pilgrims on 2 August 1715. Today its kitchen turns out nearly 3 lakh a day on average, using gram flour, ghee, sugar, cashews, raisins and cardamom.

In 2009, the Tirupati laddoo got a geographical indication, or GI tag. That legal label means only the temple can sell a laddoo under that name, the same kind of protection given to Darjeeling tea and Basmati rice. For many pilgrims, carrying a box of Tirupati laddoos home to relatives is part of the trip.

